#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""repo-tidy: remove shipped git worktrees and branches across many repos.

Dry run by default: it prints the plan and changes nothing. --apply does the
removals, behind four gates on every item:

  shipped      its work landed: tip is an ancestor of the base branch, or a
               merged PR carries exactly this tip, or its PR closed unmerged
               more than 14 days ago and the commit is on the remote
  clean        no tracked edit and no untracked file that is not gitignored
  not in use   no live process cwd or open file under it, no tmux pane, no
               file touched in the last 2 hours, no lease file naming it
  restorable   not a detached HEAD holding commits on no remote ref

Sibling slices of the same project go too, by the same four gates: a worktree
folder, a stale registration whose folder is gone, and a standalone clone in a
sibling folder whose origin remote is this project's (the old `repo-<ticket>`
folders). The main checkout is never a candidate, and a folder owned by another
repo is never touched.

A repo mid-rebase or mid-merge is skipped whole. Nothing is deleted under
~/.local/state/vcc-evidence, and nothing outside the projects root.
--max-remove caps one run so a bug cannot empty a repo.

--quick reads only local git, asks GitHub nothing and writes nothing: it prints
one TIDY line per cleanup still owed. drain-check runs it, so a merge that left
its worktree behind is a finding, not a memory test.

--prune-caches and --clean-tmp are the fast hygiene pass, for disk rather than for
git. They read no PR, fetch nothing and take seconds:

  --prune-caches   remove rebuildable build output (target/, node_modules/, .next/,
                   dist/, .turbo/) from this project's own folders that nobody has
                   touched for 48 hours. A folder with a live process, a tmux pane or
                   a lease is left alone, and so is any output directory holding a
                   git-tracked file. A worktree's files keep their commit; only what
                   a build regenerates goes.
  --clean-tmp      remove this project's own /tmp entries older than 48 hours, named
                   <project>- or <project>., that no live process holds open.

Both are dry runs without --apply, obey --max-remove, and stop at --time-budget
seconds (default 20) so a call cannot hang a chat.

Usage:
  repo-tidy [--apply] [--all | --project DIR | REPO_PATH...] [--json] [--quick]
            [--mine SESSION_CWD --branch BRANCH]
            [--prune-caches] [--clean-tmp] [--tmp-root DIR] [--time-budget SECONDS]
            [--max-remove N] [--projects-root DIR] [--jobs N]
"""

